---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import AvatarBuild from "../../components/UnderTheHood/AvatarBuild.astro"
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components"
---

<TechniquePost slug="avatar-squircles">
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      <code>border-radius</code> only draws round arcs. A squircle, the smooth superellipse
      you know from app icons, used to need an SVG mask or a <code
        >clip-path</code
      > path. Both are fixed shapes: they don't follow the element's size, and they
      clip borders and shadows along with everything else.
    </p>
    <p>
      An avatar also has to hold a photo, initials or an icon, and stay square
      whatever you put in it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-trick">The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>aspect-ratio: 1</code>: set the width, the height follows
      </li>
      <li>
        The photo is absolutely positioned with <code>object-fit: cover</code>,
        so it never sizes the avatar
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>:has(img)</code> drops the fallback color behind the photo
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>corner-shape: squircle</code> changes how the corner is drawn, <code
          >border-radius</code
        > still sets how big it is. A full radius gives a full squircle
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`.avatar {
  aspect-ratio: 1;
  border-radius: var(--radius-round);
  overflow: clip;
}

.avatar:has(img) {
  background-color: transparent;
}

.avatar.squircle {
  border-radius: var(--radius-3);

  @supports (corner-shape: squircle) {
    border-radius: var(--radius-round);
    corner-shape: squircle;
  }
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
    <AvatarBuild />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fallback">Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Without <code>corner-shape</code>, the <code>@supports</code> block is skipped
        and the squircle becomes a rounded square
      </li>
      <li>
        The full radius sits inside the block, because on its own it would draw
        a circle
      </li>
      <li>
        Without <code>:has()</code>, the background color stays behind the
        photo. It only shows through transparent images
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
